Let me write a good routine they can do at a desk.

1. Chin tucks — 10 slow reps.
2. Neck rotation — look left 20s, right 20s.
3. Side bend (ear to shoulder) — 20s each side, no hand pulling.
4. Levator scapulae / nose-to-armpit diagonal — 20s each.
5. Shoulder rolls / shrugs — 30s.
6. Chest opener in doorway — 30s.

Plus one line: the biggest cause is posture, and a note about which is the actual fix. Keep the message tight but complete.

Also it might be worth writing it into a workspace file so they can keep it? They asked for it here. Keep it in the message; optionally save to workspace. I'll save it to a file and give the path — that's the convention for long results. It's short enough for a message honestly. But saving is cheap and lets them re-find it. I'll do a file write and mention the path.

Let me write routine carefully with durations totaling ~5 min.

Routine:
Minute 1 — settle and chin tucks. Sit tall, chin gently back (make a double chin, not a nod), hold 3s, 10 reps.
Minute 2 — rotation. Turn head slowly left as far as comfortable, hold 5s, back through centre to right, 5s hold. Repeat ~3 each side. Don't force.
Minute 3 — side bend. Ear to shoulder, let the weight of head do it, 20-30s each side. Don't pull with hand.
Minute 4 — diagonal (levator). Turn head 45° toward armpit side, look down, gently nod, 20-30s each side.
Minute 5 — shoulders. Shrugs up 5s down 5s x3, then shoulder rolls back 10, then doorway chest opener 30s.

Do twice a day, morning and mid-afternoon. Warm shower first helps.

Red flags: numbness/tingling into the arm or hand, weakness, dizziness, severe headache, after a fall/whiplash — see a professional rather than stretch.

Also: a genuinely sore neck after sleeping badly may need a better pillow. Keep brief.

