args: {"text": "Five minutes, twice a day. Slow and gentle — nothing should hurt.\n\n1. Chin tucks — 1 min. Sit tall, draw your chin straight back (double chin, not a nod down). Hold 3s, release, 10 reps. This is the one that actually fixes desk posture.\n2. Turn and look — 1 min. Slowly turn left as far as comfortable, hold 5s, back through centre, right, hold 5s. About 3 each way.\n3. Ear to shoulder — 1 min. Tip your head toward one shoulder, let its weight do the work, don't pull. 20–30s each side.\n4. Look into your armpit — 1 min. Turn 45 degrees, look down toward the armpit, small nod. 20–30s each side; you'll feel it in the back of the neck.\n5. Shoulders — 1 min. Three 5-second shrugs, 10 backward rolls, then forearms on a door frame, lean through for 30s to open the chest.\n\nTwo things that matter more than the stretches: top of your screen at eye level, and if you wake up sore, suspect the pillow first. The relief from stretching lasts about twenty minutes; the chin tucks and the doorway stretch are the ones that change anything.\n\nSaved as neck-routine.md in the workspace if you want it later.\n\nAnd a line worth knowing: numbness or tingling into the arm or hand, weakness, dizziness, or a sudden severe headache means see someone — that's not a stretching problem."}
