2027-01-04T15:45:16  message   To the owner:
A Roth IRA is a retirement account you put money into after you've already paid tax on it.

The gist:

- You contribute money you've already been taxed on. No deduction now.
- It grows tax-free, and when you take it out in retirement, you pay nothing on the growth either. That's the whole appeal.
- To get that tax-free treatment, the account has to be open five years and you have to be 59½ or older. Withdraw earlier and the growth gets taxed (plus a penalty, with exceptions for things like a first home or disability).
- Your contributions — what you put in, not the growth — you can pull back out anytime, tax and penalty free. That's what makes people use it as a backup emergency fund.
- Annual limits, and income limits: above a certain income you can't contribute directly and have to go the backdoor route. Worth checking the current numbers when it matters, since they move.
- No required distributions, so unlike a traditional IRA you're never forced to withdraw.
- Nobody at work sets it up for you — you open it yourself at a broker. It's separate from a 401(k) and you can have both.

The trade-off versus a traditional IRA: traditional gives you a break now and taxes you later; Roth gives you nothing now and nothing later. If you expect to be in a higher bracket when you retire, Roth usually wins.

