2027-01-04T18:23:30  thought   A general curiosity question — give them something genuinely surprising rather than the usual 'nine brains, three hearts' list. The best candidates: their extensive RNA editing (proteins get rewritten after the gene is read, rare in animals), the distributed nervous system with tasting suckers, and dying after reproducing. Pick two or three, go a bit deep on the strangest, keep it readable. No notes change and nothing pending, so next wake goes mid-day UTC off-peak.

2027-01-04T18:23:30  message   To the owner:
Two, since they're both strange in the same direction — octopuses don't seem to be built like us at all.

The first: they edit their own RNA. Normally what your genes say is what you get — DNA is transcribed to RNA, RNA is translated into a protein, done. Octopuses have abandoned much of that rule. They have enzymes that go through the RNA copies and substitute letters before they're read, especially in the nervous system, so one gene can produce a whole family of different proteins depending on temperature and circumstances. Something like 60% of their protein-coding genes are subject to this.

The cost is speed — editing takes time, which is a terrible thing for a warm-blooded animal. The benefit is flexibility: they can tweak their own brain chemistry based on conditions instead of waiting generations for evolution. As far as we can tell they're unusually dependent on it, and some biologists think that's why their conventional genome is barely changing compared to fish or mammals — they may have painted themselves into a corner that works beautifully in cold water.

The second: their arms think for themselves. Roughly two-thirds of an octopus's neurons are in its arms rather than its brain, and each arm is a fairly capable local system — a severed arm will still reach for food and try to pass it toward a mouth that isn't there. The suckers also taste by touching, which means it's walking about receiving a stream of flavour information that only partly reports back.

Which adds up to an animal that has a mind distributed through eight limbs that aren't quite under central control, in place of a skeleton. There's footage of them solving something awkward by letting an arm find its own way there.

And then they die young: almost all species breed once and then rapidly decline — stop eating, go pale, waste away over weeks. It's driven by a gland near the eye; cut it out and they live on and eat again. There's something distinctly odd about a creature that intelligent being built to fall apart as soon as it reproduces.

