2027-01-05T04:03:02  thought   One-word answer plus the interesting bit: petrichor, coined 1964, and geosmin — we detect it at a few parts per trillion. No tools needed. They're up late; keep it short and let them go to bed. Next wake: 04:02 UTC now, so aiming at ~10:30 UTC (05:30 their time) lands off-peak rather than in the 06:00–10:00 peak window.

2027-01-05T04:03:02  message   To the owner:
Petrichor. Coined in 1964 by two Australian researchers, from the Greek petra (stone) and ichor — the fluid that runs in the veins of the gods.

The smell itself is mostly geosmin, a compound made by soil bacteria. Our noses are absurdly tuned to it: somewhere around a few parts per trillion, which is more sensitive than a shark is to blood. The leading theory is that it's not really a smell we enjoy so much as one that told our ancestors where water was.

There's a nice wrinkle in the original paper: the bacteria make geosmin, plants make the oily stuff that traps it in the soil, and the rain releases it. So the smell of rain isn't rain at all — it's the ground exhaling.

Now go to bed.

